511,-7.152555099310121e-07,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-0.0122537175193429,-0.0022512348368763924,-2.264974000354414e-06,0.0,-1.2278481335670222e-05,-4.6491513785440475e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,279,19703,315,198,78045,89,61,18,284,220,17,488,220,17,72,59,60,1371,400,64,62,16,488,18668,62,16,4779,400,64,62,17,488,18668,62,17,4779,323,400,64,62,18,488,18668,62,18,2418,220,22603,400,64,62,16,264,62,17,264,62,18,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet the roots of\n\\[z^3 = 2 + 2i\\]be $a_1 + ib_1,$ $a_2 + ib_2,$ and $a_3 + ib_3.$  Compute $a_1 a_2 a_3.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"ff005de370cff678c70e2b6ba7f332ae5f3edfb147f2e3fb77cf55d8c9c46af5"},"seconds":67.68599700927734,"seed":726075174903448287,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we will find the roots of the equation \\(z^3 = 2 + 2i\\) and subsequently calculate the product \\(a_1 a_2 a_3\\), where \\(a_1, a_2, a_3\\) are the real parts of the roots.\n\nFirst, we express \\(2 + 2i\\) in polar form. The modulus \\(r\\) and argument \\(\\theta\\) of \\(2 + 2i\\) are given by:\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{2^2 + 2^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 4} = \\sqrt{8} = 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{2}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}(1) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(2 + 2i\\) can be written as:\n\\[\n2 + 2i = 2\\sqrt{2} \\left(\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{4} + i\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(z\\) in the equation \\(z^3 = 2\\sqrt{2} \\left(\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{4} + i\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\). To find the roots, we use De Moivre's theorem. The magnitude of \\(z\\) is:\n\\[\n|z| = \\sqrt[3]{2\\sqrt{2}} = \\sqrt[3]{2^{3/2}} = 2^{1/2} = \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThe arguments of the roots are given by:\n\\[\n\\theta_k = \\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{2k\\pi}{3} \\quad \\text{for} \\quad k = 0, 1, 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the roots \\(z_k\\) are:\n\\[\nz_k = \\sqrt{2} \\left( \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{2k\\pi}{3}\\right) + i\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{2k\\pi}{3}\\right) \\right) \\quad \\text{for} \\quad k = 0, 1, 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the real parts of these roots. Using the sum-to-product identities, we can write the real parts as:\n\\[\na_k = \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{2k\\pi}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThus, the real parts \\(a_1, a_2, a_3\\) are:\n\\[\na_1 = \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right), \\quad a_2 = \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right), \\quad a_3 = \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\nNow, we compute the product \\(a_1 a_2 a_3\\):\n\\[\na_1 = \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 1\n\\]\n\\[\na_2 = \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{12}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\na_3 = \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{19\\pi}{12}\\right)\n\\]\n\nUsing the symmetric properties of cosine and the fact that the product of cosines of angles equally spaced around the unit circle is related to the polynomial coefficients, we find:\n\\[\na_1 a_2 a_3 = 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{12}\\right) \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{19\\pi}{12}\\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven the symmetry and periodicity of cosine function, the product simplifies to:\n\\[\na_1 a_2 a_3 = \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\cos\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{12}\\right) \\cdot \\cos\\left(\\frac{19\\pi}{12}\\right) = 2 \\cdot (-1) = -2\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{-2}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
